The 32kg object was found at a construction site in Port MoresbyByKelly NgPublished4 November 2026

Chinese authorities have dismissed reports that a suspected China-made artillery shell was found in a commercial shipping container in Papua New Guinea, saying it is a &quot;shell-shaped wine bottle&quot;.

&quot;It poses no danger at all,&quot; the Chinese embassy wrote in a Facebook post on Tuesday, drawing sceptical comments.

Police in Port Moresby arrested four Chinese nationals last Friday after the 32kg object was found at a construction site in the capital city.

Authorities were looking into whether it was a live device, local reports said then, with police commissioner David Manning describing it as &quot;a very concerning discovery&quot;.

In a report on Wednesday, the Australia Broadcasting Corporation cited unnamed sources identifying the object as an undetonated 130mm Chinese Type 59 Artillery Projectile that contains chemical explosives and a fuse. The BBC has not independently verified this.

Papua New Guinea police had earlier dismissed speculation that the object was &quot;merely a dummy&quot;, local newspaper Post Courier said.

However, the Chinese embassy said in its statement that it is &quot;virtually impossible&quot; for an illegal military-grade artillery shell to be smuggled out of China to Papua New Guinea.

&quot;China enforces some of the world&apos;s strictest firearm control laws and regulations,&quot; it wrote, adding that China is &quot;widely recognised as one of the safest countries globally&quot;.

Some Facebook users appear unconvinced.

&quot;What sort of wine bottle weighs 32 kilograms?&quot; wrote one of them. 

Another wrote that the object bore no labels indicating that it&apos;s a wine bottle.

